{"name":"Banyan Serenity","hex":"#98ab8c","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"banyan","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An Indian trader, merchant, cashier, or money changer.","A tropical Indian fig tree, Ficus benghalensis, that has many aerial roots."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/banyan"},{"term":"serenity","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The state of being serene; calmness; peacefulness.","A lack of agitation or disturbance."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/serenity"}],"entries":[{"title":"Banyan","blocks":[{"text":"A banyan, also spelled banian, is a fig that develops accessory trunks from adjacent prop roots, allowing the tree to spread outwards indefinitely. This distinguishes banyans from other trees with a strangler habit that begin life as an epiphyte, i.e. a plant that grows on another plant, when its seed germinates in a crack or crevice of a host tree or edifice. Banyan often specifically denotes Ficus benghalensis, which is the national tree of India, though the name has also been generalized to denominate all figs that share a common life cycle and used systematically in taxonomy to denominate the subgenus Urostigma."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Big_Banyan_Tree_at_Bangalore.jpg/330px-Big_Banyan_Tree_at_Bangalore.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Banyan"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Banyan_tree_at_Naples_FL_preserve.jpg/500px-Banyan_tree_at_Naples_FL_preserve.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Banyan_tree_at_Naples_FL_preserve.jpg/1280px-Banyan_tree_at_Naples_FL_preserve.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4